Prometheus77 <aaronsama@gmail.com> wrote:

Sorry for the following somewhat exaggerated text, its only not to
spread misinformation about the C language.

> My assumption is that
> the "L" at the end of a calculation (as in tmp+= tmp / 3L)
> rounds DOWN to an integer ... is that correct?

No, the rounding you observed is done by the "/" operator. The "L"
just promotes the type of "3" from "int" to "long int" (which is
superfluous here but does not harm).

Given non-negative values (nethack avoids negative values in
divisions) the result of the division for integer types (such as
"int" or "long") is the truncated mathematical result.

> If that is the
> case, a barbarian with CHA 7 attempting who picks up a scroll of
> scare monster (base price 100) may get charged: 100 base price
> +1/3 random markup, rounded down to 133
> +1/2 charisma markup, rounded down to 199

Nearly, to be exact (is that a pun?) its derived by:
  100
 + 33 (100/3, truncated)
 + 66 (133/2, truncated)

> as opposed to 100 * 4/3 * 3/2 = 200, right?

A slightly different calculation, but the result again is 199.
But beware: 100 * (4/3) * (3/2) would be 100 (sic!).

